      "publication_date": "2005-12-01",
      "abstract": "Click to increase image sizeClick to decrease image size Notes Notes 1. In this context see Benjamin's discussion on the concept of \u201cinvoluntary memory\u201d in Proust (Benjamin 1996 Benjamin, Walter. 1996. Selected, Writings, Volume 1 \u2013 1913\u20131926, Cambridge, MA: Belknap, P of, Harvard, UP. Trans. Rodney, Livingstone et al [Google Scholar], 207). 2. Freud develops the concept of trauma in several places, including: Beyond the Pleasure Principle, The Trauma of War, and Moses and Monotheism. 3. The French translation was authorized by Benjamin and differs in several places from the existing English and Hebrew versions. 4. In addition to historians, one can point to photographers, psychoanalysts and collectors as \u201cartisans of historical recognition.\u201d 5. The dramatic events at the start of the Second World War have left various marks upon this essay, which was written by Benjamin in 1940. Gershom Scholem interpreted it as a response to the Molotov\u2013Ribbentrop pact between Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union in 1939, and many have seen it as Benjamin's response to Stalinism, which both he and Brecht viewed as a \u201cbetrayal\u201d of the oppressed (Monnier 1991 Monnier, Adrienne. 1991. \u201cUn portrait de, Walter, Benjamin.\u201d. In \u00c9crits fran\u00e7ais (Walter, Benjamin), Paris: Gallimard. [Google Scholar], 335). In his own article on this essay, Stephan Mos\u00e8s describes the responsibility of Benjamin's historian in terms of moral obligation, although the term obligation doesn\u2019t appear in Benjamin's works (Mos\u00e8s 1993 Mos\u00e8s, St\u00e9phane. 1993. \u201cThe, Theologico-Political, Model of, History of, Walter, Benjamin.\u201d. Zemanim, 38: 24\u201346. (in, Hebrew) [Google Scholar], 7). 6. See Benjamin's essay \u201cThe Author as Producer\u201d (Benjamin 1999b Benjamin, Walter. 1999b. \u201cThe, Author as, Producer.\u201d. In Selected, Writings, Volume 2 \u2013 1927\u20131934, Edited by: Rodney, Livingstone, Trans. Cambridge, MA: Belknap, P, of, Harvard, UP. et al [Google Scholar]). 7. In the introduction to a selection of Benjamin's works edited by herself, Hannah Arendt writes about the acoustic dimension in Benjamin (Arendt 1978 Arendt, Hannah. 1978. \u201cIntroduction.\u201d. In Illuminations/Walter, Benjamin, Trans. Harry Zohn, New, York: Schocken. [Google Scholar]). 8. Gilles Deleuze's discussion on the two modes of time, aion and chronos, can be read as a conceptualization of Benjamin's unorganized thinking about time (Deleuze 1969 Deleuze, Gilles. 1969. Logique du sens, Paris: Minuit. [Google Scholar]). 9. All the above quotations are from \u201cBerlin Childhood around 1900\u201d (Benjamin 2002 Benjamin, Walter. 2002. \u201cBerlin, Childhood around 1900.\u201d. In Selected, Writings, Volume 3 \u2013 1935\u20131938, Edited by: Edmund, Trans and Howard, Eiland, Jephcott. Cambridge, MA: Belknap, P, of, Harvard, UP. et al [Google Scholar], 354, 384\u201385), except for the last one taken from Benjamin (1992 Benjamin, Walter. 1992. \u201cBerlin, Childhood around 1900.\u201d. In Walter, Benjamin, Selected, Writings, Vol. 1, Hakibbutz, Hameuhad: Trans. David, Zinger. Tel, Aviv. (in, Hebrew) [Google Scholar], 60; my translation). 10. There are several versions of the text, including thirteen or eighteen theses. 11. Some of them can be traced in the two texts mentioned above, as well as in his Passages, and in the essays on Baudelaire, on the storyteller, on Eduard Fuchs, the collector and historian, on Proust's portrait, and on the mimetic faculty. 12. Benjamin based his description of the automaton in the first thesis on a text about a chess player by Edgar Allan Poe (Poe 2004 Poe, Edgar, Allan. 2004. Tales of, Edgar, Allan, Poe, New, York: Kaplan. [Google Scholar]). 13. In the context of Benjamin's automaton, J.M. Monoya mentions the automaton built by Johann Nafumok Maelzel in the second decade of the previous century, but doesn\u2019t develop the topic (Benjamin 1991 Benjamin, Walter. 1991. \u00c9crits fran\u00e7ais, Paris: Gallimard. [Google Scholar], 335). This detail is inexact historically. Maelzel built several other automatons \u2013 most important among them the metronome and the pan-harmonica \u2013 but the Turk he acquired from Kempelen's son, and he himself only operated it. An exceptional case regarding the discussion of Benjamin is Pierre Missac, who, in several places in his book Walter Benjamin's Passages, discusses the place of the machine in Benjamin's thought (Missac 1995 Missac, Pierre. 1995. Walter, Benjamin's, Passages, Edited by: Nicholsen, Trans. S.W. Cambridge, MA: MIT P. [Google Scholar]). 14. The machine's peregrinations were reported in the annals of chess: an exhibition in Paris in 1817, New York in 1826, Boston in 1827, back to Europe in 1828, a return trip to the USA in 1829, and so on. 15. The encounter between the two took place in 1809, in Schonbrunn; the \u201cTurk\u201d beat Napoleon. 16. See Benjamin's discussion of the way fascism uses technological means in \u201cThe Work of Art.\u201d 17. In their book What is Philosophy?, Deleuze and Guattari describe the plane of immanence as an unlimited, absolute plane, neither surface nor volume, a respiration that sweeps all particles in its flow, an indivisible environment, a current in which there are no presences and no transcendence (Deleuze and Guattari 1991 Deleuze, Gilles and F\u00e9lix, Guattari. 1991. What is, Philosophy?, Edited by: Hugh, Tomlinson, Trans and Burchell, Graham. New, York: Columbia, UP. [Google Scholar]). 18. See Benjamin's discussion in Passages of the letter N, which is devoted to \u201ctheoretical thoughts on consciousness\u201d (Benjamin 1989 Benjamin, Walter. 1989. Passages: Paris, capitale du, XIXe si\u00e8cle, Paris: CERF. [Google Scholar], 488). 19. Ibid. 20. The potential or the possible also plays a central role in the essay on the task of the translator \u2013 as the capacity to be translated \u2013 and in the essay on art \u2013 as the capacity to be reproduced. 21. On aesthetics and the sensory apparatus in Benjamin, see Buck-Morss (1992 Buck-Morss, Susan. 1992. \u201cAesthetics and, Anesthetics: Walter, Benjamin's, Artwork, Essay, Reconsidered.\u201d. October, 62: 3\u201341. [Crossref] , [Google Scholar]). 22. \u201cThe Work of Art in the Era of Technical Reproduction\u201d (Benjamin 2003 Benjamin, Walter. 2003c. \u201cOn the, Concept of, History.\u201d. In Selected, Writings, Volume 4 \u2013 1938\u20131940, Edited by: Edmund, Jephcott, Trans. Cambridge, MA: Belknap, P, of, Harvard, UP. et al [Google Scholar], 269). 23. Benjamin's letter to Adorno is quoted in Pierre Missac's book (Missac 1995 Missac, Pierre. 1995. Walter, Benjamin's, Passages, Edited by: Nicholsen, Trans. S.W. Cambridge, MA: MIT P. [Google Scholar], 122). 24. In certain senses this inexplicit and undeveloped statement by Benjamin can be seen as a harbinger of Lacan's discussion on the view and the voice as objects. On this discussion in Lacan, see \u017di\u017eek (1996 \u017di\u017eek, Slavoj. 1996. \u201cI Hear, You with, My, Eyes or, The, Invisible, Master.\u201d. In Gaze and, Voice as, Love, Objects, Durham, NC and London: Duke, UP. [Crossref] , [Google Scholar]). 25. See Denis Bernard's study on the researches and inventions of Albert Londe (Bernard and Guntheret 1999 Bernard, Denis and Andr\u00e9, Guntheret. 1993. L\u2019Instant r\u00eav\u00e9 \u2013 Albert, Londe, Paris: Chambon-Trois. [Google Scholar]). 26. The quote is taken from the musical King Dagobert, by Laurencin and Claireville, at the 1844 exhibition. Note Benjamin's use of a metaphor from the world of photography: the radiation or engulfment of light rays. 27. Benjamin refers to heliotropism, but it is difficult to ignore the connection between heliotropism and heliography, the name given to photography by Ni\u00e8pce, Daguerre's collaborator, and in common use until replaced by the latter term. 28. See Benjamin's treatment of this invention in Passages (Benjamin 1989 Benjamin, Walter. 1989. Passages: Paris, capitale du, XIXe si\u00e8cle, Paris: CERF. [Google Scholar], 697), as well as Michel Frizot's discussion on the relation between photography and motion (Frizot 1998 Frizot, Michel. 1998. A New, History of, Photography, Edited by: Susan, Trans, Liz, Clegg, Bennett, Crook, John and Higgitt, Caroline. Cologne: K\u00f6nemann. [Google Scholar]). 29. On the genealogy of exposure times, revolving around the case of Daguerre, see the article by Andr\u00e9 Guntheret (Guntheret 1998 Guntheret, Andr\u00e9. 1998. \u201cDaguerre ou la promptitude \u2013 Arch\u00e9ologie de la r\u00e9daction du temps de pose.\u201d. \u00c9tudes photographiques, 5: 4\u201325. [Google Scholar]). 30. In this context see Cathy Caruth's discussion on Freud (Caruth 1996 Caruth, Cathy. 1996. Unclaimed, Experience: Trauma and the, Possibility of, History. Baltimore: Johns, Hopkins, UP. [Google Scholar]). 31. Here again Gilles Deleuze's discussion on the singular point is very close to Benjamin's discussion on the instantaneous snapshot (Deleuze 1969 Deleuze, Gilles. 1969. Logique du sens, Paris: Minuit. [Google Scholar]). 32. In his various studies, Michel Frizot attempts to deconstruct the necessary connection between the work of inventors such as Marey, Muybridge, Londe and others and the invention of the cinema. He contends that Muybridge's pursuit of a series of images of motion, at least during a certain period (1875\u201385), is not identical to the pursuits of people such as Ducos du Hauron, Cook or Bonelli, which were directed toward the invention of the cinema. See Frizot (1998 Frizot, Michel. 1998. A New, History of, Photography, Edited by: Susan, Trans, Liz, Clegg, Bennett, Crook, John and Higgitt, Caroline. Cologne: K\u00f6nemann. [Google Scholar], 244\u201345). 33. Claude Lanzman's conception, which underlies his movie Shoa \u2013 and which is guided by the imperative not to understand \u2013 comes close at this point to Benjamin's conception. 34. A comparison can be struck between Benjamin's conception and Deleuze's discussion on the concepts of repetition and difference. 35. The elimination of the epic element must be taken into account, as accomplished by Marx as the author of Das Kapital. Marx recognized the fact that the history of capital could not be processed other than within the rigorous, comprehensive framework of theory. The interests of humanity are better looked after by means of the theoretical matrix of labor under the control of capital, which Marx presents in his work, than in the monumental or long-winded, and ultimately nonchalant, volumes of historicism. (Benjamin 1991 Benjamin, Walter. 1991. \u00c9crits fran\u00e7ais, Paris: Gallimard. [Google Scholar], 356) 36. See primarily his essays on art, the task of the translator, and Baudelaire. 37. Rolf Tiedemann also points out that Benjamin's angel has nothing to say. He also quotes Kaiser, who describes the angel as someone who has no message because \u201che is the message\u201d (Tiedemann 1983\u201384 Tiedemann, Rolf. 1983\u201384. \u201cHistorical, Materialism or, Political, Messianism? An, Interpretation of the, Theses \u2018On the, Concept of, History.\u2019\u201d. The, Philosophical, Forum, XV, 1\u20132 [Google Scholar], 76). 38. See the correspondence between Benjamin and Gershom Scholem regarding the dictation (Scholem 1987 Scholem, Gershon. 1987. Story of a, Friendship, Tel, Aviv: Am, Oved. (in, Hebrew) [Google Scholar]), as well as Missac's discussion on the subject (Missac 1995 Missac, Pierre. 1995. Walter, Benjamin's, Passages, Edited by: Nicholsen, Trans. S.W. Cambridge, MA: MIT P. [Google Scholar]). 39. See my discussion on the two forms of tradition in my forthcoming book (Azoulay 2006 Azoulay, Ariella. 2006. Once, Upon a, Time: Photography after, Walter, Benjamin, UP: Ramat, Gan Bar, Ilan. forthcoming [Google Scholar]). 40. On the ethics of the spectator that can be developed out of Benjamin's mistake, see my discussion of images from the Israeli occupation of Palestinian territories (Azoulay 2004 Azoulay, Ariella. 2004. Everything, Could be, Seen, Exhibition catalogue, Gallery of, Um el-Fahem. Available <rellyaz@netvision.net.il> [Google Scholar]). 41. Benjamin quotes the surrealist poet Aragon in \u201cThe Author as Producer\u201d (Benjamin 1999b Benjamin, Walter. 1999b. \u201cThe, Author as, Producer.\u201d. In Selected, Writings, Volume 2 \u2013 1927\u20131934, Edited by: Rodney, Livingstone, Trans. Cambridge, MA: Belknap, P, of, Harvard, UP. et al [Google Scholar], 779). 42. One ear may appear different and more tattered than the other because the angel belongs to the class of clerks, who transmit words from place to place with a pencil perched over one ear, the shape of which changes over time. Such a description of an ear can be found in the short story \u201cThe Man in the Crowd\u201d by Edgar Allan Poe, one of Benjamin's heroes (Poe 2004 Poe, Edgar, Allan. 2004. Tales of, Edgar, Allan, Poe, New, York: Kaplan. [Google Scholar], 392). 43. See <http://www.icomos.org/hague/hague. convention.html> and <http://www.unhchr.ch/html/menu3/b/92.htm>.",
